# Every generated path below is written WITHOUT a trailing slash, deliberately.
# A trailing slash restricts the pattern to directories, and a symlink is not a
# directory to git however it resolves — so `node_modules/` leaves a symlinked
# `node_modules` untracked-but-visible, which is how one was committed in #1557.
# Sandbox worktrees symlink the install in rather than repeat it (the pre-push
# hook refuses to run lint or format without `$REPO_ROOT/node_modules`), so that
# symlink is the normal case here, not an accident. None of these names is one
# the repo would want tracked as a file either, so the slash bought nothing.

# Dependencies
node_modules

# Embedded server database (pglite data dir)
.verity-data
.verity-sessions

# Build output
dist
*.tsbuildinfo

# Expo / React Native (generated; native dirs are produced by prebuild/EAS)
.expo
apps/*/android
apps/*/ios

# Test/coverage
.cache
coverage

# Env / secrets
.env
.env.local
!.env.example
*.local.yml

# Local runner/onboarding state
.verity-onboarding

# Editor / OS
.DS_Store
*.log

# Agent code review marker (local review receipt written by `verity-code-review
# mark`; a per-checkout receipt, never committed — see AGENTS.md).
.agents/.last-code-review-sha
# Scratch copies of `verity-code-review` output, which the tool writes to a temp dir it
# deletes on exit. Staged in the worktree only so a session can read its own findings.
.agents/review-capture/

# Worktree re-registration sidecar: a per-checkout copy of the facts needed to
# rebuild `.git/worktrees/<name>` after a cross-namespace `git worktree prune`
# deletes it (see worktree.ts). Local to the checkout, never committed. The
# `.tmp` is what it is written as before being renamed into place.
.verity-worktree.json
.verity-worktree.json.tmp

# Core dumps. A sandbox that runs out of memory drops one of these in the
# worktree root, where `git add -A` will happily stage 800 MB of process memory
# — worker environment, gateway bearers, prompt text and all. Anchored: unanchored
# these would also swallow any `core/` directory anywhere in the tree. Anchoring
# does not distinguish a file from a directory — git has no file-only syntax — so a
# top-level `core/` would still need `!/core/` added here if one is ever introduced.
# Every suffix requires a digit, never a bare `*`: `/core.*` would also swallow a
# root-level `core.ts` or `core.json` and hide real source from `git add`. A digit
# is a weaker requirement than it looks — glob `*` matches whatever trails it, so
# `/core.*.[0-9]*` also covers a hypothetical `core.spec.1.ts`. That is accepted:
# the alternative is enumerating dump suffixes a host may not use, and no such
# source file exists at the repo ROOT, which is the only place these are anchored.
# Two
# shapes, because `kernel.core_pattern` differs per host: `core.<pid>` (the kernel
# default) and `core.<comm>.<pid>[.<time>]` (what most distro patterns produce, and
# the one that would otherwise slip through — `core.node.1234` starts with neither
# `core.` + digit nor any tracked extension).
/core
/core.[0-9]*
/core.*.[0-9]*
